<hr>
<h3 style='margin-top:16px;color:#1e1b4b;border-bottom:1px solid #ddd;'>Section A: Multiple Choice Questions</h3><p style='font-style:italic;color:#555;'>Note: Choose the correct answer for each of the following questions.</p><div style='margin-bottom:10px;'><strong>Q1.</strong> The refractive index of a medium with respect to air is given by which of the following expressions?</div><ul style='list-style-type:none;padding-left:15px;margin-top:4px;'><li style='margin-bottom:2px;'>(a) <span style="display:inline-block;vertical-align:-0.4em;text-align:center;margin:0 2px;"><span style="border-bottom:1px solid #000;display:block;padding:0 2px;font-size:0.9em;">speed of light in medium</span><span style="display:block;padding:0 2px;font-size:0.9em;">speed of light in air</span></span></li><li style='margin-bottom:2px;'>(b) <span style="display:inline-block;vertical-align:-0.4em;text-align:center;margin:0 2px;"><span style="border-bottom:1px solid #000;display:block;padding:0 2px;font-size:0.9em;">speed of light in vacuum</span><span style="display:block;padding:0 2px;font-size:0.9em;">speed of light in medium</span></span></li><li style='margin-bottom:2px;'>(c) speed of light in air &times; speed of light in medium</li><li style='margin-bottom:2px;'>(d) <span style="display:inline-block;vertical-align:-0.4em;text-align:center;margin:0 2px;"><span style="border-bottom:1px solid #000;display:block;padding:0 2px;font-size:0.9em;">1</span><span style="display:block;padding:0 2px;font-size:0.9em;">speed of light in medium</span></span></li></ul><div style='margin-bottom:10px;'><strong>Q2.</strong> A ray of light passes undeviated through a parallel glass slab when the angle of incidence is:</div><ul style='list-style-type:none;padding-left:15px;margin-top:4px;'><li style='margin-bottom:2px;'>(a) 0°</li><li style='margin-bottom:2px;'>(b) 30°</li><li style='margin-bottom:2px;'>(c) 45°</li><li style='margin-bottom:2px;'>(d) 60°</li></ul><div style='margin-bottom:10px;'><strong>Q3.</strong> The SI unit of power of a lens is:</div><ul style='list-style-type:none;padding-left:15px;margin-top:4px;'><li style='margin-bottom:2px;'>(a) Metre</li><li style='margin-bottom:2px;'>(b) Centimetre</li><li style='margin-bottom:2px;'>(c) Dioptre</li><li style='margin-bottom:2px;'>(d) Watt</li></ul><div style='margin-bottom:10px;'><strong>Q4.</strong> A convex lens forms a real, inverted image of the same size as the object when the object is placed at:</div><ul style='list-style-type:none;padding-left:15px;margin-top:4px;'><li style='margin-bottom:2px;'>(a) Focus F<sub>1</sub></li><li style='margin-bottom:2px;'>(b) Between F<sub>1</sub> and 2F<sub>1</sub></li><li style='margin-bottom:2px;'>(c) At 2F<sub>1</sub></li><li style='margin-bottom:2px;'>(d) Beyond 2F<sub>1</sub></li></ul><div style='margin-bottom:10px;'><strong>Q5.</strong> The critical angle for a glass-air interface is approximately:</div><ul style='list-style-type:none;padding-left:15px;margin-top:4px;'><li style='margin-bottom:2px;'>(a) 24°</li><li style='margin-bottom:2px;'>(b) 42°</li><li style='margin-bottom:2px;'>(c) 48°</li><li style='margin-bottom:2px;'>(d) 90°</li></ul><div style='margin-bottom:10px;'><strong>Q6.</strong> A virtual, erect, and magnified image is formed by a convex lens when the object is placed:</div><ul style='list-style-type:none;padding-left:15px;margin-top:4px;'><li style='margin-bottom:2px;'>(a) Between optical centre and focus</li><li style='margin-bottom:2px;'>(b) At principal focus</li><li style='margin-bottom:2px;'>(c) At 2F<sub>1</sub></li><li style='margin-bottom:2px;'>(d) At infinity</li></ul><div style='margin-bottom:10px;'><strong>Q7.</strong> The relationship between real depth, apparent depth, and refractive index (&mu;) is:</div><ul style='list-style-type:none;padding-left:15px;margin-top:4px;'><li style='margin-bottom:2px;'>(a) &mu; = <span style="display:inline-block;vertical-align:-0.4em;text-align:center;margin:0 2px;"><span style="border-bottom:1px solid #000;display:block;padding:0 2px;font-size:0.9em;">Apparent Depth</span><span style="display:block;padding:0 2px;font-size:0.9em;">Real Depth</span></span></li><li style='margin-bottom:2px;'>(b) &mu; = <span style="display:inline-block;vertical-align:-0.4em;text-align:center;margin:0 2px;"><span style="border-bottom:1px solid #000;display:block;padding:0 2px;font-size:0.9em;">Real Depth</span><span style="display:block;padding:0 2px;font-size:0.9em;">Apparent Depth</span></span></li><li style='margin-bottom:2px;'>(c) &mu; = Real Depth &times; Apparent Depth</li><li style='margin-bottom:2px;'>(d) &mu; = Real Depth + Apparent Depth</li></ul><div style='margin-bottom:10px;'><strong>Q8.</strong> A ray of light passing through which point of a spherical lens goes undeviated?</div><ul style='list-style-type:none;padding-left:15px;margin-top:4px;'><li style='margin-bottom:2px;'>(a) Focus</li><li style='margin-bottom:2px;'>(b) Optical Centre</li><li style='margin-bottom:2px;'>(c) Center of Curvature</li><li style='margin-bottom:2px;'>(d) Principal Axis</li></ul><h3 style='margin-top:16px;color:#1e1b4b;border-bottom:1px solid #ddd;'>Section B: Assertion-Reason</h3><p style='font-style:italic;color:#555;'>Note: Select the correct option based on the Assertion (A) and Reason (R) statements provided.</p><div style='margin-bottom:10px;'><strong>Q1.</strong> Assertion (A): A ray of light passing through the optical centre of a thin lens suffers no deviation.\nReason (R): Near the optical centre, the two surfaces of the lens are thin and parallel, acting like a thin glass plate.</div><ul style='list-style-type:none;padding-left:15px;margin-top:4px;'><li style='margin-bottom:2px;'>(a) Both Assertion and Reason are true and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion.</li><li style='margin-bottom:2px;'>(b) Both Assertion and Reason are true but Reason is NOT the correct explanation of Assertion.</li><li style='margin-bottom:2px;'>(c) Assertion is true but Reason is false.</li><li style='margin-bottom:2px;'>(d) Assertion is false but Reason is true.</li></ul><div style='margin-bottom:10px;'><strong>Q2.</strong> Assertion (A): A concave lens always forms a virtual, erect, and diminished image of an object.\nReason (R): A concave lens is a converging lens.</div><ul style='list-style-type:none;padding-left:15px;margin-top:4px;'><li style='margin-bottom:2px;'>(a) Both Assertion and Reason are true and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion.</li><li style='margin-bottom:2px;'>(b) Both Assertion and Reason are true but Reason is NOT the correct explanation of Assertion.</li><li style='margin-bottom:2px;'>(c) Assertion is true but Reason is false.</li><li style='margin-bottom:2px;'>(d) Assertion is false but Reason is true.</li></ul><h3 style='margin-top:16px;color:#1e1b4b;border-bottom:1px solid #ddd;'>Section C: Very Short Answer</h3><p style='font-style:italic;color:#555;'>Note: Answer the following questions briefly.</p><div style='margin-bottom:10px;'><strong>Q1.</strong> State Snell's law of refraction and write its mathematical expression.</div><div style='margin-bottom:10px;'><strong>Q2.</strong> Define the terms: (i) Critical angle, and (ii) Total internal reflection.</div><div style='margin-bottom:10px;'><strong>Q3.</strong> A lens has a focal length of -20 cm. Find its power in Dioptres and state the type of lens.</div><div style='margin-bottom:10px;'><strong>Q4.</strong> State two main differences between a convex lens and a concave lens based on their action on a parallel beam of light and their relative thickness.</div><div style='margin-bottom:10px;'><strong>Q5.</strong> Why does a pencil immersed partially in water appear bent at the water surface? Name the phenomenon responsible for this observation.</div><h3 style='margin-top:16px;color:#1e1b4b;border-bottom:1px solid #ddd;'>Section D: Short Answer</h3><p style='font-style:italic;color:#555;'>Note: Answer the following questions in detail.</p><div style='margin-bottom:10px;'><strong>Q1.</strong> Answer the following questions related to light passing from glass to air:</div><ol style='padding-left:20px;margin-top:4px;'><li>A ray of light passes from glass (&mu; = 1.5) into air. Calculate the critical angle for the glass-air interface given \sin(41.8^&deg;) = 0.667.</li><li>State two necessary conditions for total internal reflection to occur.</li></ol><div style='margin-bottom:10px;'><strong>Q2.</strong> An object is placed at a distance of 30 cm in front of a convex lens of focal length 20 cm.</div><ol style='padding-left:20px;margin-top:4px;'><li>Calculate the position of the image formed.</li><li>Find the linear magnification produced by the lens.</li><li>State three characteristics of the image formed.</li></ol><div style='margin-bottom:10px;'><strong>Q3.</strong> Draw a neat, labelled ray diagram showing the refraction of a monochromatic ray of light through a glass prism. Clearly mark the following:</div><ol style='padding-left:20px;margin-top:4px;'><li>Angle of incidence (i)</li><li>Angle of emergence (e)</li><li>Angle of deviation (&delta;)</li></ol><div style='margin-bottom:10px;'><strong>Q4.</strong> A convex lens forms a real image four times magnified on a screen placed at a distance of 60 cm from the lens.</div><ol style='padding-left:20px;margin-top:4px;'><li>Find the object distance from the lens.</li><li>Calculate the focal length of the convex lens.</li></ol><div style='margin-bottom:10px;'><strong>Q5.</strong> Define the principal focus for:</div><ol style='padding-left:20px;margin-top:4px;'><li>A convex lens.</li><li>A concave lens.</li><li>State the sign convention used for focal length in both cases.</li></ol><h3 style='margin-top:16px;color:#1e1b4b;border-bottom:1px solid #ddd;'>Section E: Long Answer</h3><p style='font-style:italic;color:#555;'>Note: Answer the following question completely with diagrams and proper explanations.</p><div style='margin-bottom:10px;'><strong>Q1.</strong> A convex lens has a focal length of 15 cm.</div><ol style='padding-left:20px;margin-top:4px;'><li>Draw ray diagrams to show the formation of image when the object is placed at:\n(i) A distance of 30 cm from the lens.\n(ii) A distance of 10 cm from the lens.</li><li>For each of the above cases, state three characteristics of the image formed.</li><li>Mention one practical optical instrument where each of these two arrangements is used.</li></ol>
</body>
</html>